> I need to do an ethics self-assessment for a grant proposal. Here is a short description of the proposed work:
> ===
> The PANACEA project aims to address the complex, interconnected challenges of climate risks and
> adaptation by harnessing the strengths of multiple methodologies. Traditional approaches to understanding
> climate impacts are often operate in silos; process-based models, econometric techniques, and
> macroeconomic frameworks each have independent histories. This separation has led to gaps in our
> understanding of crucial risks, particularly concerning the potential and limitations of climate risk adaptation.
> PANACEA will provide actionable information on how adaptation investments can achieve societal goals.
> Using innovative machine learning and Bayesian techniques, PANACEA will bridge these disparate
> methodologies, focusing on four critical climate risks. The project will offer new insights into losses from
> flooding, biodiversity loss, perennial crop damages, and macroeconomic growth. For each risk, key
> dimensions of adaptation will be explored; specifically, examining the roles of land use change, protected
> areas, agricultural inputs, and supply chains. These four risk areas and adaptation actions serve as powerful
> case studies for a broader interdisciplinary enhancement of our understanding of climate risks. Overall,
> PANACEA highlights the interaction between investments and risks across sectors and scales, with
> significant consequences for vulnerable communities.
> The project also seeks to utilise these new outcomes to support decision-making in low- and lower-middle-
> income countries, where the evidence base can be significantly improved by integrating across disciplines.
> Through co-design workshops, PANACEA will develop interactive tools for Nigeria and Brazil,
> incorporating targeted scenarios and information on the local consequences of development, adaptation, and
> mitigation actions. These tools will enable decision-makers to explore outcomes and design policy actions
> for both the core PANACEA sectors and the broader evidence base concerning risks and adaptation.
> ===
> 
> The things that flagged the need for a self-assessment are activities outside of the EU, and particularly in an LLMIC (Nigeria). For more information, here are the relevant work packages that would involve that work:
> 
> ===
> WP1. Stakeholder Co-design
> OBJECTIVES: Ensure that stakeholder views are incorporated into the project-wide analysis through scenario and question development, identification of outcomes of interest, and engagement around results and policy directions.
> 
> PANACEA is a stakeholder-oriented project, with frequent interaction with local stakeholders to shape the project analysis and outcomes. The primary stakeholders for PANACEA are national-level policymakers, including representatives from Ministries of Finance and Environmental Agencies. These will form (1) country-specific and project-wide advisory groups, and the core of (2) stakeholder engagement workshops occurring regularly during the project (see Gantt Chart). Building on the PI's existing relationships, the stakeholders will include:
> UK agency representatives for His Majesty's Treasury (HMT), Office of Budget Responsibility (OBR), and the Department for Environment, Food & Rural Affairs (Defra).
> Nigeria representatives of National Space Research and Development Agency (NASRDA) and the Food and Nutrition Technical Assistance Project (FANTA).
> Representatives from the Brazilian Ministry of Finance and the Novo Brasil initiative.
> 
> METHODS: The project-wide advisory group will include representatives from the (three) country-specific advisory groups. Each of the four advisory groups will meet quarterly virtually, and in-person workshops will be convened twice for each country (the PI has organised two large workshops on improving climate risk assessments, leading to Rising et al. 2022 and Royal Society 2023). Key decision points include:
> Project-wide decisions: Identify climate scenarios and global economic baselines: The PI has used off-the-shelf Shared Socioeconomic Pathways (SSPs), Network for Greening the Financial System (NGFS) scenarios, and probabilistic Resources for the Future Socioeconomic Projections (RFF-SPs) in past work, but stakeholder engagement under a current project suggests that policymakers are more interested in "most likely" and "worst case" scenarios.
> Country-specific decisions: (1) Develop risk and adaptation storylines: Domestic and international storylines for each country will provide context and applications for the quantitative risk outcomes and adaptation models (WPs 2-5 and WP6). These will be developed through workshops. Meetings will also discuss the significance of results to active policy discussions. (2) Design interactive visualisations to inform decision-making: Interactive displays will allow stakeholders to explore risks across dimensions of region, time, scenario, and sector, as well as the benefits of adaptation across solutions and investment plans. Workshops will be used to design and refine interfaces to the project dataset.
> 
> WP6. Impacts Synthesis
> OBJECTIVES: Provide total economic risk estimates at a subnational level and develop an interactive tool for decision-makers to explore adaptation investment pathways.
> 
> The outputs of this project, combined with empirical results from the Climate Impact Lab and biophysical process model results from IIASA, offer a potential sea change in the assessment of vulnerabilities in poor and data-poor regions. Two interfaces will be developed based on discussions with WP1 LLMIC stakeholders, building on an existing model that the PI is developing for the UK. The tool will allow decision-makers to develop "adaptation pathways" (Werners et al. 2021) and visualise the costs and benefits of different adaptation investment plans.
> 
> METHODS (collaborator Nicola Ranger, LSE):
> Develop emulators of outcomes from PANACEA and from collaborating groups: This work builds upon three existing projects by the PI: (1) a project within the Climate Impact Lab to emulate risk relationships, (2) a Climate and Clean Air Coalition (CCAC) report to emulate climate risks from the broader literature, and (3) near-comprehensive estimates of climate risk in the United Kingdom (Rising et al. 2022c (public report), under review at Nature Climate Change). These light weight emulators reproduce risk and adaptation results from existing models, allowing them to be projected under custom scenarios.
> Integrate emulators into the Policy Analysis of the Greenhouse Effect (PAGE) model: The PAGE model (Waidelich et al. 2024) is a state-of-the-art integrated assessment model that incorporates risks, adaptation, and mitigation, which has been integrated with the FaIR climate model (Leach et al. 2021) and disaggregated to the country level under the CCAC project. Interactions between adaptation, mitigation, development, catastrophic change, and a range of policy levers will be explored under the new WP1 scenarios.
> Develop an interface for WP2-5 data and for creating adaptation pathways: Through workshops coordinated with WP1, we will develop two new interfaces on an existing decision-support tool to identify risks globally and allow stakeholders to explore adaptation investment plans.
> ===
